  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nMid and upper level low pressure system slowly rotates over the\nnortheast coast this afternoon gradually shifting east this\nevening with drier air pushing in behind it. Isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to linger into\nthe afternoon before tapering off. Instability also increases \ntowards the afternoon hours allowing additional showers and \nthunderstorms to develop over the area. Instability parameters \nare modest with ML CAPE values up to 500 J/Kg at most, along \nwith 20 knots of shear. It remains humid with dew points in the \nmid to upper 60s and pwats around 1.3 inches. We can't rule out \nsome brief locally heavy rainfall with the storms this \nafternoon, but overall severe weather is not expected today. \nHighest PoPs are over the Catskills of east-central NY. The rain\nshowers end tonight and skies partially clear. Winds turn light\nas high pressure gradually builds in from the west. This will \nbe a good setup to see valley fog development. Lows dip down \ninto the mid-50s and low 60s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith high pressure in place Friday through Saturday conditions \nremain generally dry. There is a slight chance for a late day shower \nor thunderstorm to pop up over the Catskills where the slow \ndeparting trough will still hold some influence. Otherwise \ntemperatures warm into the upper 70s to mid-80s, with generally \ncomfortable humidity levels. Similar conditions are expected \nSaturday with mostly dry conditions in the morning and afternoon \nhours. As an upper level ridge moves in on Saturday, southwesterly \nflow settles into the region. As a result, temperatures trend \nslightly warmer with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s over the \nregion. Dry conditions will be short lived as another frontal system \napproaches the region in the evening and overnight hours increasing \nprecipitation chances.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nBy Sunday, the next deep trough, front and low pressure center \nwill be moving into the area from the midwest. With southwesterly\nflow out ahead of it, abundant moisture gets advected into the \nregion increasing PWATs 1.8 to 2.0 inches. Model consensus is \ngood on the arrival timing and position of these features. It is\ntoo early for specific details on exactly how much instability \nwill be present, as that will certainly depend on day time \nsurface heating. The main concern is the risk for excessive \nheavy rainfall as this system moves through. Model guidance does\nshow some timing differences in when the system will exit. GFS \nkeeps the upper level low rotating over the east coast through \nWednesday, while the ECMWF and Canadian show it moving east \nsometime Monday with an upper level ridge building in behind. \nBeyond this, expect a warming trend for the middle of next week \nwith chances for afternoon and evening thunderstorms also in the\nforecast.",
